00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:06.060
We've talked about how vectorization lets you speed up your code significantly.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.820
In this video, we'll talk about how you can vectorize your implementation of logistic

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:15.120
regression so that you can process an entire training set that is implement a single iteration

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.940
of gradient descent with respect to an entire training set without using even a single

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.340
explicit for loop.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.620
I'm super excited about this technique and when we talk about neural networks later without

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.340
using even a single explicit for loop.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.900
Let's get started.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.100
Let's first examine the four propagation steps of logistic regression.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.700
So if you have m training examples, then to make a prediction on the first example, you

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:47.300
need to compute that, compute z using the familiar formula, then compute the activation,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.940
then compute y hat in the first example.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.860
Then to make a prediction on the second training example, you need to compute that.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.300
Then to make a prediction on the third example, you need to compute that and so on.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.020
You might need to do this m times if you have m training examples.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.500
So it turns out that in order to carry out the four propagation step, that is to compute

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.980
these predictions on all m training examples, there is a way to do so without needing an

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.500
explicit for loop.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.820
Let's see how you can do it.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.900
First remember that we defined the matrix capital X to be your training inputs, you

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.060
know, stacked together in different columns like this.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:38.540
So this is a matrix that is a nx by m matrix.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:46.180
So I'm writing this as a Python NumPy shape, but this just means that your X is a nx by

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.420
m dimensional matrix.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:56.780
Now the first thing I want to do is show how you can compute z1, z2, z3 and so on

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.460
all in one step in fact with one line of code.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:11.940
So I'm going to construct a 1 by m matrix, this is really a row vector, where I'm going to

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:19.300
compute z1, z2 and so on down to zm all at the same time.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:27.700
It turns out that this can be expressed as W transpose, the capital matrix X plus an index

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:38.500
vector b, b and so on, b where this thing, this b, b, b, b, b thing is a 1 by m vector

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:46.940
or 1 by m matrix or that is a m dimensional row vector.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:53.660
So depending on how familiar you are with matrix multiplication, you might see that W transpose

00:02:53.660 --> 00:03:05.930
X1, X2 and so on through Xm that W transpose is going to be a row vector.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.890
So W transpose will be a row vector like that.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:24.890
And so this first term will evaluate to W transpose X1, W transpose X2 and so on, dot dot dot W transpose

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.610
Xm.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:32.890
And then when you add this second term b, b, b, b and so on, you end up adding b to each

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:33.890
element.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.610
So you end up with another 1 by m vector where that's the first element, that's the second

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.130
element and so on and that's the nth element.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:50.170
And if you refer to the definitions above, this first element is exactly the definition of

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:57.490
Z1, the second element is exactly the definition of Z2 and so on.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:04.010
So just as X was what you obtained when you took your training examples and stacked them

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:11.130
next to each other, stacked them horizontally, I'm going to define capital Z to be this,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.090
where you take the lowercase z's and stack them horizontally.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:21.330
Okay, so when you stack the lowercase x's corresponding to different training examples,

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.090
horizontally you get this variable capital X.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.210
So in the same way when you take these lowercase z variables and stack them horizontally, you

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:34.130
get this variable which you're going to know by capital Z.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:40.690
And it turns out that in order to implement this, the numpy command is capital Z equals

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:51.290
np dot W dot t as W transpose X and then plus b.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:56.890
Now there is a subtlety in Python which is that here b is a row number or if you want

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:01.570
to say, you know, one by one matrix, it's just a normal row number.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:08.490
But when you add this vector to this row number, Python automatically takes this row number

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:13.370
b and expands it out to this one by m row vector.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:19.250
So in case this operation seems a little bit mysterious, this is called broadcasting in

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.610
Python and you don't have to worry about it for now, we'll talk about it some more

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.010
in the next video.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:30.330
So the takeaway is that with just one line of code, with this line of code, you can calculate

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:36.930
capital Z and capital Z is going to be a one by m matrix that contains all of the lowercase

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:41.850
z's, lowercase z1 through lowercase zm.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:42.850
So that was z.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.890
How about these values, you know, a, right?

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:57.770
What we'd like to do next is find a way to compute a1, a2, and so on through am all at

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.170
the same time.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:06.650
And just as stacking lowercase x's resulted in capital X and stacking means stacking horizontally

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:12.370
lowcase z's results in capital Z stacking lowercase a's is going to result in a new variable

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:15.690
which we'll define as capital A.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:22.890
And in the program assignment, you see how to implement a vector valued sigmoid function.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:30.930
So that the sigmoid function inputs this capital Z as a variable and very efficiently outputs

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:37.010
capital A. So you see the details of that in the program assignment.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:42.690
So just to recap, what we've seen on this slide is that instead of needing to loop over

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:50.850
m training examples to compute lowercase z and lowercase a one at a time, you can implement

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.610
this one line of code to compute all the z's at the same time.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:07:01.090
And then this one line of code with appropriate implementation of lowercase sigma to compute

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.290
all the lowercase a's all at the same time.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:08.890
So this is how you implement a vectorized implementation of the forward propagation

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.690
for all m training examples at the same time.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:16.930
So to summarize, you've just seen how you can use vectorization to very efficiently compute

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:21.690
all the activations, all the lowercase a's, so that at the same time.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:27.850
Next, it turns out you can also use vectorization to very efficiently compute the backward propagation

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.730
to compute the gradients.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.570
Let's see how you can do that in the next video.
